<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savang Vatthana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–c. 1980</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savang Vatthana was the last King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a> in 1959. His reign coincided with civil war between the royal government and the communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, and with heavy American bombing. After the Pathet Lao took power he abdicated on 2 December 1975, and he and his family were later sent to a re-education camp, where he is believed to have died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savang-vatthana.jpg" alt="Savang Vatthana" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Savang_Vatthana_1959.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Savang Vatthana was born on 13 November 1907, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a>, and was a cousin of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souvanna-phouma">Souvanna Phouma</a>. At ten he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he attended a lycée in Montpellier and then the École Libre des Sciences Politiques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After a decade abroad he is said to have been unable to speak Lao. He married a cousin, Khamphoui, and unlike his polygamous father took only one wife; they had five children. Under an arrangement made with the French in 1941 he became chairman of the King's Council, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> overthrew the French administration in March 1945 he protested against the Japanese actions at their headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his father in 1959 but was never formally crowned, postponing the ceremony until the end of the war. He was devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and guarded a sacred Buddha image, believing that as long as it was in his hands the country was safe. Time wrote in 1961 that his most striking political characteristic was a profound lethargy.</p><p>After a coup by Captain Kong Le in August 1960, rival governments claimed office, and after the 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> agreements on neutrality <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souvanna-phouma">Souvanna Phouma</a> headed a coalition. In February 1963 the king visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>. A reviewer of Christopher Kremmer's history of the dynasty wrote that in the 1960s he allowed the United States to bomb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a>-held territory in the northeast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 29 November 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souvanna-phouma">Souvanna Phouma</a> and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a> persuaded the king to abdicate, and the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed on 2 December, with the former king as supreme adviser to the president. In 1977 he, the queen and the crown prince were moved to Camp 01 in Houaphan province. The government confirmed his death only in December 1989, and accounts of the date differ; one account, drawn on a former prisoner's memoir, places it about two months after the crown prince's death. The royal palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> was preserved largely as he had left it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sisavang Vatthana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>King Savang Vatthana</b> abdicated on 2 December 1975, and the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Pathet Lao gained control of the coalition government formed under the 1973 accords, <b>King Savang Vatthana</b> abdicated, and on 2 December 1975 the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed in Vientiane, with Prince Souphanouvong as president and Kaysone Phomvihane as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in Xam Neua, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1954" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Savang Vatthana"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1954">What was here in 1954? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
